uncovered, you know, in our perception of other people, we're taking in this huge volume of information. Our minds are unconsciously processing the majority of it, and a very small sliver seems to get promoted up to our conscious thought. And that conscious thought is sometimes misdirected, as we talked about with bias. It can lead us down paths that we wouldn't ideally like to take. And so the big question that I am still pursuing is kind of that one that I started with, which is what, what is it that we know versus we don't know? How do we reconcile those two things? Why do we say things that are different from what we do? And making sense of all of that, particularly as it applies to our perceptions of each other. Right now, one of the directions that my lab is taking with this,
我们发现，在对其他人的感知中，我们正在吸收巨大体量的信息。我们的大脑在无意识地处理其中的大部分，而只有极小的一部分似乎能够上升到我们的意识思维中。而这种意识思维有时会被误导，就像我们之前讨论过的偏见一样。它可能会把我们引向我们本不愿选择的道路。因此，我仍在追求的重大问题依然是我最初开始时的那个，那就是：我们所知道的和我们所不知道的之间究竟是什么关系？我们如何调和这两者？为什么我们说的话和做的事会不一样？并理解所有这一切，特别是当它应用在我们对彼此的感知上时。目前，我的实验室正在朝着这个方向发展，

